WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.790
SPEAKER: The best way to
maximize cost optimization

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.700
for Google Kubernetes Engine
is to make sure your app

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.520
and GKE are configured
for each other.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.830
Today, we'll talk about
some tips and best practices

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.140
for configuring GKE
to work with your app,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.079
and we'll wrap up our
cost optimization journey.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.880
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:25.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.890
The last video went over some
tips for configuring your app.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.690
And the videos
before that covered

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.370
a lot of best practices.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.090
If you haven't seen
the previous videos,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.840
make sure to watch them
before you watch this one.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.240
I'll start with some suggestions
for batch and serving

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:39.050
workloads.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.240
Then, we'll do some
networking, and finally,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.970
wrap up the whole video series
and talk about what's next.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.790
As always, knowledge
about your application

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.610
can help you focus on which
cost-optimization techniques

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.680
will give you the most impact.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.360
We can simplify
most applications

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.400
by grouping them
into two groups--

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.590
batch and serving.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.780
Batch applications
have work to do,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.100
but tend to be less
concerned about startup time

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.450
and usually aren't
time-sensitive overall.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.090
Apps like monthly data
calculations or low priority

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.690
data transformation are
great examples of batch jobs.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.430
As these jobs need
to be scheduled,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.590
the cluster autoscaler
can spin up new nodes,

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.990
and then remove them
when the job is done.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.000
Even better, node
autoprovisioning

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.220
works best with batch
workloads since it

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.810
can figure out
optimal resources,

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.330
and then create a new node
pool, and doesn't need to worry

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.400
as much about spikes in demand.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.400
Here's some other tips for
working with batch workloads.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.560
The cluster autoscaler
can delete empty nodes

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.960
faster when it doesn't
need to also restart

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.060
the pods on those nodes.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.970
Splitting up your applications
into dedicated node pools,

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.970
using labels and selectors
or taints and tolerations,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.490
can help the cluster
autoscaler scale quicker

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.650
and avoid wasted resources.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.050
There's even an
optimize-utilization profile

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.390
for the cluster autoscaler
that focuses more

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.030
on maximizing utilization
rather than keeping

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.830
spare resources at the ready.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.350
It scales down a lot quicker,
which can help aggressively

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.690
minimize extra resources.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.820
Also, you can separate
different applications

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.890
with node auto-provisioning,
along with using

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.479
taints and tolerations.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.510
Node pools will then be better
sized for each application,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.450
once again, optimizing
your resource usage.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.670
And don't forget that
preemptible VMs are

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.390
much cheaper to use, but only
if your apps are fault-tolerant

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.900
and much less time-sensitive.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.550
Opposite of batch
applications are

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.370
serving apps, which need
the ability to scale rapidly

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.800
in response to
spikes in demand--

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.310
for example, a retail website
on major shopping holidays.

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.960
In order to handle the
sudden increases in demand,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.510
it's good for serving
workloads to be

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.600
lean and optimized to
work best with GKE.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.220
The quicker that
pods can start up,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.308
the more efficient autoscalers
are at responding to demand.

00:02:46.308 --> 00:02:48.350
If you have a serving app
that requires resources

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.960
with a long provisioning
time, like, GPUs,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.940
it makes sense to
overprovision and have

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.060
resources at the ready
before the demand spikes.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:59.000
Plus, you can use pause pods
to help you scale ahead.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.710
We went over some
ways to overprovision

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.500
and to keep your apps lean
in the previous videos.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.120
Optimizing both of
those will give you

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.140
the most cost-efficient way
to handle high-demand spikes.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.210
Here's another best
practice for GKE.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.000
Make sure to use
NodeLocal DNSCaching.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.980
If you're running a
version with kube-dns,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.050
apps that make a
lot of DNS lookups

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.910
will put extra pressure on it.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:21.500
While the kube-dns
autoscaler will

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.660
create more kube-dns
replicas, it

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.440
takes time to add those replicas
and increases resource usage.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.780
Instead of just increasing
the kube-dns replicas

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.890
in your configuration, add
the NodeLocal DNSCache add-on.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.390
Each node will then
store a DNS cache,

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:38.870
which helps kube-dns
require less resources

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.650
and saves overall
time for lookups.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.830
On top of that, round-trip
request time is usually better

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.000
when it's low.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.760
One tip for lowering this is
to use container-native load

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.870
balancing.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.990
Rather than sending requests to
instance groups and then pods,

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:55.870
container-native load balancing
allows requests to go directly

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.840
to the pod by creating what's
called a Network Endpoint

00:03:58.840 --> 00:03:59.800
Group.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.870
When these are
configured, GKE Ingress

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.790
will handle the
creation of the L7 load

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.010
balancer, along with
forwarding rules, health

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.140
checks, firewalls, and more.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.660
One quick tip for you-- if
you do container-native load

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.510
balancing, you'll want to start
failing your readiness probe

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.842
as soon as you receive
a SIGTERM, which

00:04:15.842 --> 00:04:17.800
we talked a little bit
about in the last video.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.480
That way the load
balancers will know

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.510
to stop forwarding new
requests to your app.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.400
Not only does container-native
load balancing

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.790
provide improved load balancing
and more efficient autoscaling,

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:29.890
it can also enable you
to use other tools, like,

00:04:29.890 --> 00:04:33.010
Traffic Director, and other
Google Cloud services.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:36.250
All right, I know this was a
ton of information to absorb.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.870
You can always
reference these videos

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.963
and the full best
practices guide, which

00:04:39.963 --> 00:04:42.130
will be kept up to date as
new versions and tips are

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:42.910
available.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.730
Plus, those tips lead
right to the documentation

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.080
for steps on how
to implement them.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.290
Let's do a quick
summary and checklist.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:52.960
Start with the culture and
move the cost discussion

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:53.920
to the left.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.488
Help teams understand how
their work affects cost,

00:04:56.488 --> 00:04:58.780
even if you've built an
abstraction layer between teams

00:04:58.780 --> 00:04:59.860
and their clusters.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.080
Try creating programs,
like, online classes

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.120
to help raise
familiarity and create

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.520
cost-optimization awareness.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.200
Use monitoring to
better understand

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.450
how applications use
resources, and then

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.490
implement quotas,
requests, and limits,

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:14.830
that make sense for your
apps, while also minimizing

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.660
any wasted costs.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.820
From machine families
to preemptibles,

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.620
you can optimize
your infrastructure

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.760
to improve performance
and save money.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.450
Don't forget about committed
use discounts, too.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.490
The cloud's dynamic
resources help

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.650
make autoscaling an
efficient strategy.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.930
But configuring it correctly
for your applications

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.210
is vital to handling
spikes in demand,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:37.110
while also balancing
performance with cost.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.610
Make sure that you spend time
to increase your application's

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.890
compatibility with
Kubernetes and configuring

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.310
GKE for your application
for the best results.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.460
Cost optimization is all about
increasing the business value

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.490
for what you get out of what
you're spending on the cloud.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.320
Saving costs can
be a part of that,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.150
but these tips are
more about getting

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:58.870
the most return out of costs
and optimizing your usage.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.550
We've covered a
wide range of things

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.303
to consider when building,
scaling, and deploying on GKE.

00:06:04.303 --> 00:06:06.220
Make sure to check out
the full best practices

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:07.300
guide for more details.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.425
And thank you for watching.

00:06:08.425 --> 00:06:17.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

